#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575671} :activity/id #uuid "a1366fcc-47d7-51a2-a862-4ff17c7553b7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050601 :topic/id #uuid "577aebce-a9af-5bcd-867f-fb32f559c852" :topic/math-academy-id 2049} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575575 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71387c0f-e6a7-5b6e-9830-dfc20f995ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)^{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575156} {:answer/id #uuid "76bf41af-9ab4-574b-8dd0-f707aa39bbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{4}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575167} {:answer/id #uuid "8fce1628-d8e0-5281-b8ed-e11ff1078647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{3}(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575196} {:answer/id #uuid "bb97bf22-330e-53ac-a29c-edfed6b0497b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=(x+1)(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575284} {:answer/id #uuid "bc7e7c1b-a02d-5475-921c-16202d65baa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{9}(x+1)^{2}(x-3)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575287} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ba9f72b-e89c-5805-b07f-6b4475a07e83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186575435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8f422c3e-86d1-54ac-8460-f66bdadcbf77" :question/math-academy-id "q-38766" :question/problem "![](images/92/9263d7e8f4e23d1713234db1fb92d0ba48aeeb723b3085e72c5932aff7239790.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the equation of the polynomial curve shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function has precisely two distinct roots, $x=-1,$ and $x=3.$\n\nBased on the graph, we can make the following conclusions about the multiplicities of the roots:\n\n- Since the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=-1,$ this root has even multiplicity.\n\n- Since the graph passes through $x=3,$ this root has odd multiplicity, and since the graph is tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=3,$ the multiplicity must be greater than one.\n\nFrom the given options, the only possible equation is $y=\\frac{1}{9}(x+1)^{2}(x-3)^{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "60068a08-fa3f-50ca-8965-30a471415f04"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575659 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326706 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "834b4bf2-1b3f-5d4c-b491-7c02c93b219d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438272} {:answer/id #uuid "0d78d218-e768-5739-88e2-395aa7b97bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438273} :answer-field/id #uuid "3261d56f-16e4-5e60-975f-57584426806f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439165} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23eb9a8c-3df5-5681-8e74-07043805cab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438274} {:answer/id #uuid "049c70be-8905-50c7-afdd-44a53874517c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438275}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438274} :answer-field/id #uuid "742164e8-41f0-546c-b779-b81e59044b5b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db18b8f2-ea83-5fef-a642-7bbba6480576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438276} {:answer/id #uuid "80d5c6ad-057f-53d5-b496-f302316f61f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186438276} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fc05ecc-740b-590b-bb06-34ae388c9b15" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f729aa18-847a-50e3-929f-e0ca720e3184" :question/math-academy-id "q-38680" :question/problem "![](images/68/682105815047990048e100ea59c2652e48c0deea8c8caa422bfe3efca7d11c65.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are correct?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n- **II.** $x=-1$ is a simple root.\n- **III.** $x=2$ is a root of even multiplicity." :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves down in the negative direction towards $-\\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is false. The root $x=-1$ is not a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point, and the curve is tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement III is false. The root $x=2$ has an odd multiplicity because the graph passes through the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."} :step/id #uuid "673d9b83-3ff3-5843-9dcb-506dafb7b36d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575671 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4971ab0f-5ec5-5003-a609-5d0cf402c1a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{I}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324569} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa949b0-f597-5aa3-9c71-8403549813b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{III}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324889} {:answer/id #uuid "c4ba91b6-5698-5092-ba30-953615f1d91e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{IV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325663} {:answer/id #uuid "d733a9c7-582f-5c87-8ffe-2dbdff843439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the given graphs are correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325842} {:answer/id #uuid "e36393d5-52ce-50a1-81a7-c73a90fc7d56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{II}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325969}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186324569} :answer-field/id #uuid "de404b27-a713-5cbf-9bd5-187346f817ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "72df582a-bd3a-59c2-a7b1-3fd49a2aeb61" :question/math-academy-id "q-38631" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f801d9b46aa43cb1065c2935513aee7cc0e448378423735c05ec6df7da89ff8d.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=(x+2)(x+1)^{2}(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=-2,1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=-2,1$ are simple roots, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $2,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{I}$ and $\\text{III}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is positive, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{I}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{I}.$"} :step/id #uuid "93468536-e912-5d59-af9e-1d96c93b2485" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575659}}] :activity/title "Graphing General Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574946}]}
